"""IRS Publication 946 Appendix A — the mid-month REAL-PROPERTY percentage tables.

WHY THESE ARE TABLES AND NOT COMPUTED
=====================================
The rest of the engine computes MACRS from first principles (Pub 946 ch. 4's
"without using the tables" method), which reproduces Appendix A for personal
property to the cent. **Real property is different, and computing it is WRONG.**

The IRS did not derive the mid-month real-property rows from the exact fraction.
It rounded the MONTHLY increment first and then accumulated. For 39-year
property the monthly increment is 1/12/39 = 0.21368% -> rounded to 0.214%, and
the January year-1 figure is built up from the December half-month:

    0.107 + 11 x 0.214 = 2.461%

whereas the exact fraction 11.5/12/39 gives 2.4573%. Those are DIFFERENT
NUMBERS, and 2.461% is the published, authoritative one. On a $2,000,000
building the difference is ~$75 in year 1 — small, but it is simply the wrong
figure, and a reviewer checking against Appendix A would find it.

So for the mid-month classes the engine LOOKS UP the published percentage. The
first-principles path is not a permissible alternative here.

SOURCE
======
IRS Publication 946, Appendix A (verified against the 2022 revision, whose
Appendix A tables are unchanged across the revisions this engine supports).
Table selection follows Pub 946 Appendix A "Chart 2" (residential rental and
nonresidential real property).

VERIFICATION
============
Every table below was reconstructed positionally from the publication PDF and
then checked against an independent invariant: **each of the 12 month columns
must sum to EXACTLY 100.000%**. All 48 columns across the four tables do.
``tests/unit/test_macrs_tables.py`` re-asserts that invariant, and pins the
January year-1 39-year figure at exactly 2.461%.

Percentages are Decimal STRINGS applied to the depreciable basis. Index by
``[month_placed_in_service][year_index]`` where month is 1-12 and year_index is
0-based (0 = the first tax year).
"""
from __future__ import annotations

from decimal import Decimal


